description:
Split out of MESH-89 (item 9) on 2026-11-01. The user asked: "can we use image analysis and edge detection to give it a good base for its strokes"; "likely every shading edge should be a stroke"; and, after the first attempt, to iterate on it and to think about "another round of improving the base mesh finder since it still doesnt exactly match the pictures".

Part A: strokes from the reference pictures (the original item 9):
- place each reference in its template cell with the fitted scale and centre trace-outlines computes (ReferenceOutlines), so picture pixels land on the sculpt's own view;
- every shading edge inside the subject becomes a stroke: ridge or valley by which side is lit, radius from the edge's width, depth from its contrast;
- dark connected regions (eye sockets, nasal opening, mouth, gaps between teeth) become fills, depth from how dark they are;
- written into the sculpt's SVG in groups per reference view (edges_<view>), ready for sculpt-sync, so the sculptor edits and deletes strokes rather than typing them.

First attempt (written in the mesh-89 worktree, moved here): ShadingEdges, ReferenceStrokes, ReferenceStrokesRoute and the reference-strokes command; trace-reference --edges now traces thinned one-pixel lines (run 3's streaks came from tracing both sides of each un-thinned edge band, unsigned, at one strength). Measured on run 4's references and base: 522 strokes; chamfer 0.01983 against the base's 0.02004 (run 4's hand sculptor reached 0.0165). What is wrong, from its pictures (tmp/edges/ in this worktree):
- coverage: on the front reference the strokes cover only a strip down the middle (nose, teeth); the eye sockets, cheekbones and brow, the strongest shading in the picture, get almost none. Suspect the rule keeping each mark only from the picture that sees it most squarely;
- no cavities: 17 shallow fills in all; depth from contrast, capped by the terrace limit, never sinks a socket;
- the sculpt reads as crumpled creases over the base, not skull form;
- the right and back views gave only valleys: their light estimate could not tell ridge from valley, and an unknown sign defaults to valley.

Part B: a better base mesh. The base (trace-outlines into silhouette_hull, volume_to_mesh, keep_largest_part, smooth_mesh, quad_layout) still does not match the pictures: run 4's base scored 60.4 px summed over seven views, every view 5.6-11.4 px off, and sits inside every outline (MESH-84 holds the earlier measurements and hypotheses: strict carving of inconsistent jittered traces, rounding, smoothing shrink). Improve the base finder the way the silhouette-hull literature intends rather than adding a workaround stage, measured with outline-compare against the traced outlines of run 4's references, and tell how much of the remaining chamfer the base accounts for.

definition-of-done:
Shown as pictures on run 4's references (tmp/references) and base (tmp/base.ply), all under tmp/ and named in changes-made:
(a) per reference, its computed strokes over the picture, ridges, valleys and fills in different colours, covering the face's sockets, cheekbones, brow, nose and teeth in the front picture and the main creases in every other;
(b) all computed strokes over the base in the 8-view template (strokes_overlay.py from the stroke-sculpt skill);
(c) the base sculpted with those strokes alone, its multiview under the reference strip, with sockets and the nasal opening sunk visibly;
(d) stroke counts per view and kind;
(e) the improved base's outline-compare per view and summed, against run 4's 60.4 px, with its overlay picture;
(f) the chamfer of (c) and of the improved base, measured by the orchestrator (the goal stays out of the worktree); the target is to beat run 4's hand sculptor (0.0165) with computed strokes alone.
Unit tests for the conversion on hand-made pictures (no mesh files) and for any base-finder change; the full suite passes.
